INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_STORAGE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The operation state is not valid (it must be active), or\0A *         the library has not been previously initialized by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_mac_update(psa_mac_operation_t *operation,\0A                            const uint8_t *input,\0A                            size_t input_length);\0A\0A/** Finish the calculation of the MAC of a message.\0A *\0A * The application must call psa_mac_sign_setup() before calling this function.\0A * This function calculates the MAC of the message formed by concatenating\0A * the inputs passed to preceding calls to psa_mac_update().\0A *\0A * When this function returns successfully, the operation becomes inactive.\0A * If this function returns an error status, the operation enters an error\0A * state and must be aborted by calling psa_mac_abort().\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning Applications should not call this function if they expect\0A *          a specific value for the MAC. Call psa_mac_verify_finish() instead.\0A *          Beware that comparing integrity or authenticity data such as\0A *          MAC values with a function such as \5Cc memcmp is risky\0A *          because the time taken by the comparison may leak information\0A *          about the MAC value which could allow an attacker to guess\0A *          a valid MAC and thereby bypass security controls.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam[in,out] operation Active MAC operation.\0A * \5Cparam[out] mac          Buffer where the MAC value is to be written.\0A * \5Cparam mac_size          Size of the \5Cp mac buffer in bytes.\0A * \5Cparam[out] mac_length   On success, the number of bytes\0A *                          that make up the MAC value. This is always\0A *                          #PSA_MAC_LENGTH(\5Cc key_type, \5Cc key_bits, \5Cc alg)\0A *                          where \5Cc key_type and \5Cc key_bits are the type and\0A *                          bit-size respectively of the key and \5Cc alg is the\0A *                          MAC algorithm that is calculated.\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS\0A *         Success.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL\0A *         The size of the \5Cp mac buffer is too small. You can determine a\0A *         sufficient buffer size by calling PSA_MAC_LENGTH().\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_STORAGE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The operation state is not valid (it must be an active mac sign\0A *         operation), or the library has not been previously initialized\0A *         by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_mac_sign_finish(psa_mac_operation_t *operation,\0A                                 uint8_t *mac,\0A                                 size_t mac_size,\0A                                 size_t *mac_length);\0A\0A/** Finish the calculation of the MAC of a message and compare it with\0A * an expected value.\0A *\0A * The application must call psa_mac_verify_setup() before calling this function.\0A * This function calculates the MAC of the message formed by concatenating\0A * the inputs passed to preceding calls to psa_mac_update(). It then\0A * compares the calculated MAC with the expected MAC passed as a\0A * parameter to this function.\0A *\0A * When this function returns successfully, the operation becomes inactive.\0A * If this function returns an error status, the operation enters an error\0A * state and must be aborted by calling psa_mac_abort().\0A *\0A * \5Cnote Implementations shall make the best effort to ensure that the\0A * comparison between the actual MAC and the expected MAC is performed\0A * in constant time.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam[in,out] operation Active MAC operation.\0A * \5Cparam[in] mac           Buffer containing the expected MAC value.\0A * \5Cparam mac_length        Size of the \5Cp mac buffer in bytes.\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS\0A *         The expected MAC is identical to the actual MAC of the message.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_SIGNATURE\0A *         The MAC of the message was calculated successfully, but it\0A *         differs from the expected MAC.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_STORAGE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The operation state is not valid (it must be an active mac verify\0A *         operation), or the library has not been previously initialized\0A *         by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_mac_verify_finish(psa_mac_operation_t *operation,\0A                                   const uint8_t *mac,\0A                                   size_t mac_length);\0A\0A/** Abort a MAC operation.\0A *\0A * Aborting an operation frees all associated resources except for the\0A * \5Cp operation structure itself. Once aborted, the operation object\0A * can be reused for another operation by calling\0A * psa_mac_sign_setup() or psa_mac_verify_setup() again.\0A *\0A * You may call this function any time after the operation object has\0A * been initialized by one of the methods described in #psa_mac_operation_t.\0A *\0A * In particular, calling psa_mac_abort() after the operation has been\0A * terminated by a call to psa_mac_abort(), psa_mac_sign_finish() or\0A * psa_mac_verify_finish() is safe and has no effect.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam[in,out] operation Initialized MAC operation.\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The library has not been previously initialized by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_mac_abort(psa_mac_operation_t *operation);\0A\0A/**@}*/\0A\0A/** \5Cdefgroup cipher Symmetric ciphers\0A * @{\0A */\0A\0A/** Encrypt a message using a symmetric cipher.\0A *\0A * This function encrypts a message with a random IV (initialization\0A * vector). Use the multipart operation interface with a\0A * #psa_cipher_operation_t object to provide other forms of IV.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam key                   Identifier of the key to use for the operation.\0A *                              It must allow the usage #PSA_KEY_USAGE_ENCRYPT.\0A * \5Cparam alg                   The cipher algorithm to compute\0A *                              (\5Cc PSA_ALG_XXX value such that\0A *                              #PSA_ALG_IS_CIPHER(\5Cp alg) is true).\0A * \5Cparam[in] input             Buffer containing the message to encrypt.\0A * \5Cparam input_length          Size of the \5Cp input buffer in bytes.\0A * \5Cparam[out] output           Buffer where the output is to be written.\0A *                              The output contains the IV followed by\0A *                              the ciphertext proper.\0A * \5Cparam output_size           Size of the \5Cp output buffer in bytes.\0A * \5Cparam[out] output_length    On success, the number of bytes\0A *                              that make up the output.\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS\0A *         Success.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_PERMITTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\0A *         \5Cp key is not compatible with \5Cp alg.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\0A *         \5Cp alg is not supported or is not a cipher algorithm.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_STORAGE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The library has not been previously initialized by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_cipher_encrypt(mbedtls_svc_key_id_t key,\0A                                psa_algorithm_t alg,\0A                                const uint8_t *input,\0A                                size_t input_length,\0A                                uint8_t *output,\0A                                size_t output_size,\0A                                size_t *output_length);\0A\0A/** Decrypt a message using a symmetric cipher.\0A *\0A * This function decrypts a message encrypted with a symmetric cipher.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam key                   Identifier of the key to use for the operation.\0A *                              It must remain valid until the operation\0A *                              terminates. It must allow the usage\0A *                              #PSA_KEY_USAGE_DECRYPT.\0A * \5Cparam alg                   The cipher algorithm to compute\0A *                              (\5Cc PSA_ALG_XXX value such that\0A *                              #PSA_ALG_IS_CIPHER(\5Cp alg) is true).\0A * \5Cparam[in] input             Buffer containing the message to decrypt.\0A *                              This consists of the IV followed by the\0A *                              ciphertext proper.\0A * \5Cparam input_length          Size of the \5Cp input buffer in bytes.\0A * \5Cparam[out] output           Buffer where the plaintext is to be written.\0A * \5Cparam output_size           Size of the \5Cp output buffer in bytes.\0A * \5Cparam[out] output_length    On success, the number of bytes\0A *                              that make up the output.\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS\0A *         Success.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_PERMITTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\0A *         \5Cp key is not compatible with \5Cp alg.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\0A *         \5Cp alg is not supported or is not a cipher algorithm.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_STORAGE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The library has not been previously initialized by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_cipher_decrypt(mbedtls_svc_key_id_t key,\0A                                psa_algorithm_t alg,\0A                                const uint8_t *input,\0A                                size_t input_length,\0A                                uint8_t *output,\0A                                size_t output_size,\0A                                size_t *output_length);\0A\0A/** The type of the state data structure for multipart cipher operations.\0A *\0A * Before calling any function on a cipher operation object, the application\0A * must initialize it by any of the following means:\0A * - Set the structure to all-bits-zero, for example:\0A *   \5Ccode\0A *   psa_cipher_operation_t operation;\0A *   memset(&operation, 0, sizeof(operation));\0A *   \5Cendcode\0A * - Initialize the structure to logical zero values, for example:\0A *   \5Ccode\0A *   psa_cipher_operation_t operation = {0};\0A *   \5Cendcode\0A * - Initialize the structure to the initializer #PSA_CIPHER_OPERATION_INIT,\0A *   for example:\0A *   \5Ccode\0A *   psa_cipher_operation_t operation = PSA_CIPHER_OPERATION_INIT;\0A *   \5Cendcode\0A * - Assign the result of the function psa_cipher_operation_init()\0A *   to the structure, for example:\0A *   \5Ccode\0A *   psa_cipher_operation_t operation;\0A *   operation = psa_cipher_operation_init();\0A *   \5Cendcode\0A *\0A * This is an implementation-defined \5Cc struct. Applications should not\0A * make any assumptions about the content of this structure.\0A * Implementation details can change in future versions without notice. */\0Atypedef struct psa_cipher_operation_s psa_cipher_operation_t;\0A\0A/** \5Cdef PSA_CIPHER_OPERATION_INIT\0A *\0A * This macro returns a suitable initializer for a cipher operation object of\0A * type #psa_cipher_operation_t.\0A */\0A\0A/** Return an initial value for a cipher operation object.\0A */\0Astatic psa_cipher_operation_t psa_cipher_operation_init(void);\0A\0A/** Set the key for a multipart symmetric encryption operation.\0A *\0A * The sequence of operations to encrypt a message with a symmetric cipher\0A * is as follows:\0A * -# Allocate an operation object which will be passed to all the functions\0A *    listed here.\0A * -# Initialize the operation object with one of the methods described in the\0A *    documentation for #psa_cipher_operation_t, e.g.\0A *    #PSA_CIPHER_OPERATION_INIT.\0A * -# Call psa_cipher_encrypt_setup() to specify the algorithm and key.\0A * -# Call either psa_cipher_generate_iv() or psa_cipher_set_iv() to\0A *    generate or set the IV (initialization vector). You should use\0A *    psa_cipher_generate_iv() unless the protocol you are implementing\0A *    requires a specific IV value.\0A * -# Call psa_cipher_update() zero, one or more times, passing a fragment\0A *    of the message each time.\0A * -# Call psa_cipher_finish().\0A *\0A * If an error occurs at any step after a call to psa_cipher_encrypt_setup(),\0A * the operation will need to be reset by a call to psa_cipher_abort(). The\0A * application may call psa_cipher_abort() at any time after the operation\0A * has been initialized.\0A *\0A * After a successful call to psa_cipher_encrypt_setup(), the application must\0A * eventually terminate the operation. The following events terminate an\0A * operation:\0A * - A successful call to psa_cipher_finish().\0A * - A call to psa_cipher_abort().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam[in,out] operation     The operation object to set up. It must have\0A *                              been initialized as per the documentation for\0A *                              #psa_cipher_operation_t and not yet in use.\0A * \5Cparam key                   Identifier of the key to use for the operation.\0A *                              It must remain valid until the operation\0A *                              terminates. It must allow the usage\0A *                              #PSA_KEY_USAGE_ENCRYPT.\0A * \5Cparam alg                   The cipher algorithm to compute\0A *                              (\5Cc PSA_ALG_XXX value such that\0A *                              #PSA_ALG_IS_CIPHER(\5Cp alg) is true).\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS\0A *         Success.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_PERMITTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\0A *         \5Cp key is not compatible with \5Cp alg.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\0A *         \5Cp alg is not supported or is not a cipher algorithm.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_STORAGE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The operation state is not valid (it must be inactive), or\0A *         the library has not been previously initialized by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_cipher_encrypt_setup(psa_cipher_operation_t *operation,\0A                                      mbedtls_svc_key_id_t key,\0A                                      psa_algorithm_t alg);\0A\0A/** Set the key for a multipart symmetric decryption operation.\0A *\0A * The sequence of operations to decrypt a message with a symmetric cipher\0A * is as follows:\0A * -# Allocate an operation object which will be passed to all the functions\0A *    listed here.\0A * -# Initialize the operation object with one of the methods described in the\0A *    documentation for #psa_cipher_operation_t, e.g.\0A *    #PSA_CIPHER_OPERATION_INIT.\0A * -# Call psa_cipher_decrypt_setup() to specify the algorithm and key.\0A * -# Call psa_cipher_set_iv() with the IV (initialization vector) for the\0A *    decryption. If the IV is prepended to the ciphertext, you can call\0A *    psa_cipher_update() on a buffer containing the IV followed by the\0A *    beginning of the message.\0A * -# Call psa_cipher_update() zero, one or more times, passing a fragment\0A *    of the message each time.\0A * -# Call psa_cipher_finish().\0A *\0A * If an error occurs at any step after a call to psa_cipher_decrypt_setup(),\0A * the operation will need to be reset by a call to psa_cipher_abort(). The\0A * application may call psa_cipher_abort() at any time after the operation\0A * has been initialized.\0A *\0A * After a successful call to psa_cipher_decrypt_setup(), the application must\0A * eventually terminate the operation. The following events terminate an\0A * operation:\0A * - A successful call to psa_cipher_finish().\0A * - A call to psa_cipher_abort().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam[in,out] operation     The operation object to set up. It must have\0A *                              been initialized as per the documentation for\0A *                              #psa_cipher_operation_t and not yet in use.\0A * \5Cparam key                   Identifier of the key to use for the operation.\0A *                              It must remain valid until the operation\0A *                              terminates. It must allow the usage\0A *                              #PSA_KEY_USAGE_DECRYPT.\0A * \5Cparam alg                   The cipher algorithm to compute\0A *                              (\5Cc PSA_ALG_XXX value such that\0A *                              #PSA_ALG_IS_CIPHER(\5Cp alg) is true).\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS\0A *         Success.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_PERMITTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\0A *         \5Cp key is not compatible with \5Cp alg.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\0A *         \5Cp alg is not supported or is not a cipher algorithm.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \